// Lint waivers for the Verilog Clash generates. Used by `make lint`.
//
// Clash zero-extends every vector index to 64 bits before indexing:
//
//   assign c$d_app_arg = $unsigned({{(64-2) {1'b0}},sel});
//   assign d = vecArray_0[c$d_app_arg];
//
// The index is provably in range -- its Haskell type is `Index 4` -- so the
// truncation reported here is a code-generation style, not a defect. The waiver
// is scoped to the generated directory, and `lint_off -match` is not supported
// in Verilator 5.052, so it cannot be narrowed to the one message text.
//
// One glob over every generated top entity rather than a line per design: the
// waiver is about how Clash indexes vectors, which is the same in all of them, and
// a list would be one more place to remember when a design is added.
// UNOPTFLAT is the same story with a version number on it. `Pmod.SdCard.crc7` is a
// `foldl` over forty bits, which Clash emits as one 280-bit wire holding all forty
// intermediate CRCs, written and read by the same generate loop:
//
//   assign ws        = {7'b0000000, ws1};   // the seed, then the intermediates
//   assign c$vec2    = ws[287-1 : 7];       // shifted by one element
//   assign ws1[i*7+:7] = f(c$vec2[i*7+:7], bit i);
//
// Element i depends on i-1 and nothing else, so the chain is acyclic -- but the
// whole word appears in its own cone, and Verilator has to split it bit-range-wise
// to see that. 5.052 does; the 5.020 that Ubuntu 24.04 ships does not, and reports
// seven of these for `sd` alone. The warning is about Verilator's own eval-loop
// scheduling and says nothing about synthesis: nextpnr routes this design and
// closes timing on it. Waived rather than pinning a Verilator in CI, which would
// cost a source build per run; revisit if apt ever catches up.
`verilator_config
lint_off -rule WIDTHTRUNC -file "*.topEntity/*.v"
lint_off -rule UNOPTFLAT -file "*.topEntity/*.v"
